TI  - Cognitive stimulation in a-MCI: an experimental study.
AU  - Moro V
AU  - Condoleo MT
AU  - Sala F
AU  - Pernigo S
AU  - Moretto G
AU  - Gambina G
PY  - 2012 Mar
JO  - American journal of Alzheimer's disease and other dementias
VL  - 27
IS  - 2
SP  - 121-30
DO  - 10.1177/1533317512441386 [doi]
AB  - Nowadays, preventing the effects of mental decline is an international priority, but there is little research into cognitive training in mild cognitive impairment (MCI). We present the results of a program aimed at teaching memory strategies and improving metacognitive abilities. This was associated with training to ameliorate caregivers' assistance. Two groups (A and B) were compared in a crossover design. After the first evaluation, group A (but not B) participated in a 6-month cognitive stimulation program. After a second assessment, only B received treatment and then a final evaluation was carried out on both the groups. The results show that (1) both the groups improved their performance as an effect of training; (2) improvements are specific to the functions trained; (3) in the interval without intervention, performance of group B worsened; and (4) group A has maintained their results over time. In conclusion, our results show that specific training may reduce memory impairment in MCI.
