AB  - Comments on an article by Jill D. Waring et al. (see record 2023-45047-001). The study by Waring et al. published in this issue represents a laudable effort to test the neural effects of an innovative intervention that combines computerized cognitive training with vortioxetine, an antidepressant shown to have cognitive enhancing properties. In a sample of healthy aging adults, the authors randomized participants to receive vortioxetine or placebo, plus computerized cognitive training, for 6 months. Waring et al. recruited a highly cognitively “normal” sample of older adults who all scored within one standard deviation of the age-normed mean on a neuropsychological composite of fluid cognition. The computerized cognitive training program used by Waring et al. used several different exercises to train a range of fluid cognitive skills. A follow-up study might test whether the effect on brain connectivity is more robust when the exercises focus on isolated cognitive functions such as cognitive control, working memory, or sustained attention. If the results of Waring et al. are replicated in a larger trial, then demographic, clinical, and baseline brain connectivity variables may all inform which individuals are most likely to benefit from combining vortioxetine with cognitive training (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
