AB  - Comments on an article by B. Wong et al. (see record 2022-51087-001). The article by attempted to identify an assessment tool that is most appropriate for detecting depression in mild cognitive impairment (MCI). In spite of a comprehensive literature search and a detailed analysis,Wong et al. In view of the complexity and heterogeneity of depression in older adults, not only do we need to be sensitive in identifying and quantifying the severity of depression cross-sectionally but also we should try to understand the longitudinal course of depression in relation to cognitive impairment. The latter is essential in helping us to differentiate which type of disorder the older person is primarily living with so that we can tailor the intervention according to the individual’s condition. Moreover, timely response to those who are found to have depression, supported by high-quality well-integrated mental healthcare and services, is crucial. CSDD is often administered to rate depression in nursing homes, yet this must be followed by appropriate after-care and regular re-assessments, not merely collected to comply with policy or funding imperatives. To assess and forget helps nobody. Furthermore, management of depression in MCI should not be limited to drug treatment, especially since older adults with neurocognitive disorders are less responsive to antidepressants and more prone to side effects and augmentation with cholinesterase inhibitors has no clear benefit. Instead, alternative treatment modalities including psychological treatments and cognitive training, which appear to be useful even in MCI with comorbid depression and a collaborative, coordinated, and personalized approach are instrumental to the management and care of these older adults. Rating scales are primarily designed to provide a quick screening or serve as a reference for documenting or monitoring symptom severity. Healthcare professionals and research staff need to be mindful about the strengths and limitations of these scales so that they can be used appropriately and effectively. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
