AB  - Comments on an article by E. Carbone et al. (see record 2019-37812-001). The pilot study by Carbone et al. assessed working memory training in 34 older adults hospitalized for knee arthroplasty − one of the most common elective operations among older adults. Those who received working memory training had broad positive benefits, beyond the treatment target, with gains in additional related cognitive domains like executive functions and reduced mood and anxiety symptoms. The Carbone paper points to the importance of targeting the treatment to a group in need; just as we would not evaluate success of antidepressants by examining change in mood scores for a nondepressed sample, we need to evaluate cognitive training paradigms in those at-risk for actual cognitive impairment. Carbone’s study is a first step in this strategy, by targeting an at-risk group. It is concluded that we should try to use additional enrichment criteria to determine success. Here is one final benefit of cognitive training: it is a dataintense intervention. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
