AB  - This study discusses potential of computerized cognitive training on HIV-associated neurocognitive disorder. This case comparison study reflects an explicit hypothesis of the parent R01 study: that dosage represents an important consideration in improving cognitive function. The participant who received 20 hours of SOP training had larger SOP gains than the participant who received 10 hours of SOP training, and both had larger gains than the control group (Case A). In addition, both cases B and C improved remarkably on the UFOV test. In the gerontology literature, improvement on this test is the hallmark of this cognitive training, which also reflects better driving ability in older adults. In conclusion, as cognitive abilities begin to decline with age, concerns emerge about more profound cognitive declines in older adults with HIV. Fortunately, prior work already shows that SOP training can improve this cognitive ability and improve everyday functioning in middle-aged (401) and older adults with HIV in the short-term, and in the long-term for normal communitydwelling older adults. Although more data are needed on a much larger sample to confirm the efficacy of this cognitive training program, this case comparison study highlights the potential impact of SOP training in addressing HAND and the many symptoms caused by poorer cognitive functioning. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
