AB  - Comments on an article by L. Chen and B. Lu (see record 2020-31478-012). Using the 6-year follow-up data, the paper by Chen and Lu examined the relationship between hearing difficulties, cognitive reserve, and incident cognitive impairment. Within the global mental health framework, comprehensive psychosocial prevention, promotion, and treatment interventions are emerging to address the impact of social determinants on individuals’ mental health. With the cognitive reserve hypothesis, the relationship between sociocultural and environmental challenges and cognitive health status has been gradually recognized at the macro level. The existing literature enlightens the potential benefit of social intervention to maintain cognitive health in the elderly. For example, encouraging older adults to be engaged in lifelong learning (e.g. attending senior school) and social life (e.g. joining community club) seems promising to raise individuals’ literacy and resilience. Sociostructural development, such as improving access to educational resources (e.g. building community libraries), intricate work (e.g. providing opportunities for intellectually demanding duty), social network facilities (e.g. creating senior day centers), and brain health promotion services (e.g. arrangement for cognitive training), may create a thriving environment for cognitive health. Generally speaking, it is promising to investigate the nexus between cognitive reserve and potentially modifiable risk factors from an epidemiological, biological, and social perspective. However, the concept and measures need further refinement. With the cognitive reserve hypothesis, social intervention seems promising to preserve cognitive health in the elderly. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
