AB  - This study conducted a preliminary investigation into data from a dual WM–physical activity cognitive training regime targeting healthy early elderly participants to explore if exercise habit modulates intervention effect. The 'SYNAPSOLOGY' intervention was weekly for 3 months with a flexible task framework – dual task or WM tasks – based on EF components with physical activity. The results suggested that intervention was more effective for all subjects regularly exercising prior to intervention, t(34) = 2.91, P = 0.006, versus the non-exercising group, t(16) = 0.78, P = 0.45. However, the exercising group pre- and post-intervention PEN scores were 5.37 and 3.43, while those for the non-exercising group were 3.29 and 2.65. Thus, non-exercising group scores were consistently numerically better despite no significant intervention effect, possibly explained by a ceiling effect. Exercising group scores suggest it is necessary to control for participant history, such as higher subjective morbidity, and family history for predisposition for dementia. Therefore, quantitative assessment of pre-intervention behavior might be crucial for clarifying intervention effects. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2018 APA, all rights reserved)
