AB  - Comments on an article by A. Camozzato et al. (see record 2015-18387-002), which investigated different social variables and their influence on the occurrence of AD in a cohort of aging Brazilians enrolled into the Porto Alegre Longitudinal Aging (PALA) study. The findings of the study are interesting and coincide with previous recognition that social isolation and loneliness are associated with depression and the development of dementia. Having a person in whom one may confide and receive emotional support is expected to reduce the sense of loneliness. It also makes sense that the identity and demographic characteristics of the confidant are not relevant to his or her influential role on the person’s well-being. Lifestyle interventions such as adopting a pet, engaging in social, physical and leisure activities and cognitive training may reduce the risk for dementia and maintain general and cognitive health. These interventions may not always be simple to achieve; however, they may require a lesser financial investment and carry less adverse effects than taking a prescription drug. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
