AB  - Developed a memory training program which takes into account the participants' different cognitive abilities and which can be applied to daily tasks. Ss were 36 residents (aged 72–93 yrs) of a senior citizens home in Germany. Using the Mini-Mental State Examination Ss were divided into a test group, consisting of 13 Ss (mean age 83.5 yrs) who participated in 12 training sessions during a 6-wk period, and a control group, consisting of 13 Ss (mean age 84.5 yrs), who gathered once a week for a discussion group. Before and after the treatment sessions, all Ss were administered (1) the Syndrom-Kurz-Test (syndrome short test; H. Erzigkeit, 1989) to assess attention and memory disorders and (2) the Brief Cognitive Rating Scale (B. Reisberg & S. Ferris, 1988) to measure the degree of dementia. The results show significant improvements among the test group with regard to attention and memory performance compared to the control group. Considering the positive, but short-lived effects of such training, a new treatment concept needs to be developed based on a continuous application of cognitive training measures. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
