AB  - Studied the serial organization of memory with the Portuguese-language List Discrimination Test in Brazil. The clinical group consisted of 14 psychiatric patients (mean age 31.86 yrs), 11 patients with multiple sclerosis (mean age 42.55 yrs), and 5 patients with frontal lobe pathology (mean age 55.40 yrs). The control group consisted of 58 university students (mean age 23.72 yrs) and 11 Ss in a cognitive training program for seniors (mean age 65.62 yrs). During stimulus presentation, 2 lists of 10 stimuli each were presented before and after a distraction task. In the testing phase, 20 pairs of stimuli (1 new and 1 previously shown) were presented. Temporal oral judgments were compared in the different groups. The findings show that all patients and all elderly Ss displayed performance dissociation with normal recognition and altered serial ordering in memory. The results suggest that the List Discrimination Test may be used to distinguish between young adults and older adults and among patients with different types of pathology. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
