AB  - Evaluated the acceptance and efficacy of cognitive training with Alzheimer patients based on reports from Alzheimer outpatients at a psychiatric hospital in Germany and their caregivers. 8 patients (5 female, 3 male; aged 51–87 yrs) participated regularly in the group training. During a 3-mo period, Ss received 1.5 hrs of training (divided in 30-min segments) once a wk. The training was based on the results of the SIMA project (W. D. Oswald, R. Rupprecht, T. Gunzelmann & K. Tritt, 1996) and consisted of both cognitive and psychomotoric excercises. At the beginning of the study period, Ss were assessed using the Mini-Mental-Status Scores (M. F. Folstein, S. E. Folstein & P. R. McHugh, 1975). Before and after each training session, Ss completed visual analog scales measuring their emotional and psychical well-being. Following the study period, relatives and patients completed questionnaires evaluating cognitive changes and their attitudes toward the group training. The results show that, despite the fact that cognitive improvement will not occur due to disease progression, the majority of Ss judged their cognitive capability as stable or improved. Both Ss and caregivers reported a positive impression toward the training. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
