AB  - Studied the influence coaches' methods and attitudes on the learning performance of students with learning disabilities. Ss were 3 adult trainers (aged 20–50 yrs) and 9 children and adolescents (aged 11–14 yrs) with learning disabilities. Ss were evaluated for verbal and nonverbal IQ, and specific and general cognitive skills before and after specialized training sessions in cognitive strategies. Coaches and Ss were observed during training sessions and coaching methods and attitudes were evaluated according to emotional acceptance, use of praise and encouragement, acceptance of students' ideas, questions, use of actual test items, use of metacognitive methods, and criticisms. The results indicate that use of direct instruction methods, training intensity, and use of metacognitive strategies are most important for successful training of children with learning disabilities. 9 hypotheses on the relation of coaches' methods and attitudes and learning performance of children were generated. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
